===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|fr|rampe}}, from {{der|en|frm|rampe}}, deverbal of {{m|frm|ramper}}, from {{der|en|fro|ramper|t=to crawl, climb, scale up}}, from {{der|en|frk|*hrampōn|t=to contract oneself, wrinkle, rumple, crumple, curve}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hrimpaną|t=to shrivel, shrink}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|Rampf|t=retraction, curvature, shrinkage, spasm}}. {{doublet|en|romp}}.

Akin also to Old English {{m|ang|ġehrimpan|t=to wrinkle, rimple, rumple}}, {{cog|goh|rimpfan}} (German {{m|de|rümpfen|t=to wrinkle up}}). Compare {{cog|da|rimpe|t=to fold" (archaic), "to baste}}, {{cog|is|rimpa}}. More at {{l|en|rimple}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|inclined surface}} An [[inclined]] [[surface]] that [[connect]]s two [[level]]s; an [[incline]].
# {{lb|en|North America}} An [[interchange]], a road that connects a [[freeway]] to a [[surface street]] or another [[freeway]].
# A [[structure]] with an inclined surface made for [[stunt]]s, as for jumping [[motorcycle]]s or other [[vehicle]]s.
# {{lb|en|aviation}} A [[mobile]] [[staircase]] that is attached to the [[door]]s of an [[aircraft]] at an [[airport]].
# {{lb|en|cricket}} A way of hitting a [[boundary]] by facing a cricket bat face front and pushing with force to launch the ball; of the [[entirety]] done against [[pace]].
#: {{ux|en| He hit three '''ramps''' in a row to push his team near the opponents total.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation}} A large parking area in an [[airport]] for [[aircraft]], for loading and unloading or for storage (see also [[apron]] and [[tarmac]]).
# {{lb|en|aviation}} A surface inside the [[air intake]] of a [[supersonic]] aircraft which adjusts in position to allow for efficient [[shock wave]] [[compression]] of incoming air at a wide range of different [[Mach number]]s.
# {{lb|en|skating}} A [[construction]] used to do skating [[trick]]s, usually in the form of part of a [[pipe]].
# A [[scale]] of [[value]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Julio Sanchez; Maria P. Canton|title=The PC Graphics Handbook|page=915
|passage=The RGB model uses the color component of light sources in order to produce more realistic and pleasant results. Internal color representations are always based on a palette-based color '''ramp'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Sam Kauffmann; Ashley Kennedy|title=Avid Editing: A Guide for Beginning and Intermediate Users|page=40
|passage=We have created a volume '''ramp'''. Play the section in the Timeline and listen to the volume change.}}
